  1780	# Chapter 29 — Listening
  1781	
  1782	He spent most of the night looking for the edges of it, and found that it had none.
  1783	
  1784	He lay on his back in the dark with the blanket up to his chin and the frost growing on the window, and turned his attention outward, onto his own skin, where the new thing lay. He could feel that it was there. That was the strange part: he could feel it the way you feel a coat you have been wearing all day, not as weight exactly, only as the knowledge that something is on you. It lay over his arms and his back and his face, very thin, and it did not move, and it did not answer.
  1785	
  1786	He tried asking it the way he asked the Wind. A drop, sharp, all at once, the way the hip went. Nothing dropped, because there was nowhere in the coat to drop from. He tried the way of the giving face: found a beat in his breathing, slow, and let it run, and waited for the new thing to come up and ride on it. Nothing rode. He tried simply wanting it, hard, with his jaw set, which had never worked on anything in his life and did not work now. Every time he leaned on it his attention slid off, like a hand on a frosted window, and he was lying in a cold room in the dark with his eyes open, being a fool.
  1787	
  1788	Toward morning he stopped, and lay there with his hands on the blanket, and thought about the Wind.
  1789	
  1790	He had forgotten how long it had taken. That was the thing he made himself remember in the dark. He had forgotten, because it had been his for so long now, how useless it had been at the start. It had come at Torvin's and then done nothing he asked for weeks, and it had come by itself on the worst mornings and never on the good ones. Lira had stood over him in the cold with a stick all that winter calling *stop* at every burst, and he had fallen on the straw more times than he had landed. It had been a whole season before he could ask it for anything and be sure of an answer. The giving face had been the same, in its way: two misfires up the wrist before it would ride a strike on the beat. Twice already he had started from nothing like this. He had only forgotten what nothing felt like.
  1791	
