  1822	"I said at the wall I'd tell you the rest of the estate wall," he said. "The yard man. The month of sitting." He turned the hand over. "I thought he'd put me there to be out of the way. While they talked about me in the long room. I hated him for it. Every morning for a month, after the station, he sat me down on the flags with my back to the wall of the kitchen garden and said *stay there till I call you*, and went in, and left me. No post. No falls. Nothing. Just the wall, and the cold, and the gardeners going by with their barrows. I was fourteen and I'd just been read Copper and everybody in the house had gone quiet, and I sat there with nothing to do but be angry."
  1823	
  1824	"And then?"
  1825	
  1826	"And then about the third week I stopped being angry, because you can't keep it up that long with nothing to feed it. I stopped listening for him to call me. I stopped listening for anything." Brom was quiet a moment. "That was the morning I first felt one of the gardeners on the other side of the wall. Through the brick. An old man with a barrow, and I could feel him going along, his weight and his tiredness and the barrow, like a man upstairs. I didn't know what it was. I nearly got up and ran."
