  1880	It came about a minute after, quite suddenly, as if it had been waiting politely at the door. It was not like the eye-ache of the gaze, which lived behind his forehead and grew slowly through a night. It was a band, and it went round his head from temple to temple behind his eyes and pulled tight, and stayed tight, and the alcove lamp became a thing he did not want to look at. He sat with his eyes shut and his palms cupped over them, keeping the lamp out.
  1881	
  1882	He knew what it was: the same thing the gaze spent, the same thing that went out of him on a long night on the east bench and came back with sleep; only the gaze took a whole bout to draw that much, and this had drawn it in four breaths. Four breaths of feeling the man had cost him what an evening of watching cost. The Wind spent his hip and his breath. The giving face spent the hollow under his ribs. This spent something he could only call his attention itself, the plain stuff he did all his looking with, and it spent it the way a crack in a jug spends water.
  1883	
  1884	"Mine costs me nothing," said Brom, from the bench. "Now. Two years of it coming out of me every day, and the bill got smaller every month." He came across and sat down on the floor in front of Cael, cross-legged, nearer than before; a forearm off, the sparring distance. "Again. When the head lets you. This time I'm not going to sit like a post. I'll shift about, slow. See if it stays on me."
