  1910	"Everything else you've got, you point," said Brom. "The step goes where you send it. The thing in your arm goes into whatever you hit. The way you watch, the hooks and the columns, that's pointing too; you aim your whole self at a man and pull the truth out of him. You've learned every one of them by aiming." He shook his head, slowly. "You'll never aim this. Try to turn it into something you aim and all you'll get is a worse pair of eyes than the ones you've already got. And you'll never find out what it was."
