  1940	*Costs: attention itself, whatever I look with. Four breaths cost what a night of the gaze costs. A band round the head a minute after, an hour or more. And it won't share. With the hip leaning, it goes. With the hollow stirring, it goes. Opening it means putting both of the others down.*
  1941	
  1942	*Open questions: Two people in a room. A crowd. Somebody who isn't kindled. Whether it gets cheaper, like his. Whether it ever runs while I move. What it's for, if it isn't for anything I point. B.: it's for knowing.*
  1943	
  1944	He read the entry through: it was the shortest *Deployment range* in the book and the longest *Open questions*. That was right; that was what the book was supposed to look like.
  1945	
  1946	Then, at the bottom, outside the fields, because there was one more true thing and he wanted it said:
  1947	
  1948	*I own something I cannot use. Twice before I've owned something I couldn't use, and both times the road from owning to using was a winter of mornings like this one, on stone, getting it wrong in front of somebody patient. I know that road now. I know what it costs a day. Begin.*
  1949	
  1950	He capped the ink.
  1951	
  1952	He sat a while with his hand on the closed book. His head had eased to a dull pressure. Through the wall he could hear Lira moving about her room, not yet asleep, not yet gone down the hill to her lamp.
  1953	
  1954	He let it drift open without meaning to. He was only sitting very still, tired, with nothing to aim at, and the thing on his skin found the stillness on its own and opened, a crack. And there through the wall, two paces off, faint, there was somebody: a light quick weight, never still for long, like a bird on a sill that keeps shifting its feet.
  1955	
  1956	He shut it.
  1957	
  1958	He shut it hard, the way you shut a letter you have found open on somebody's table and realise is not yours. The band came back round his head for his trouble. He sat with it and did not mind it.
  1959	
  1960	Then he opened the Log again, to Tuesday's page, to the narrow empty column at the edge headed *Carrying*, and after a while he wrote the first thing in it, very small.
  1961	
  1962	*L., through the wall. Didn't.*
  1963	
  1964	He would show her, since she had asked him to show her first.
  1965	
  1966	Down at the bottom of the hill, through the window over the alcoves, the practice lamp came on. He watched it for a while. On Monday Dace would chalk the main season on the wall by the slate, and the floors would fill, and somebody would want him on their card who had not wanted him a week ago. He had three things that were not a Path now, and one of them he could not use in any room with more than one person in it. He had a friend whose whole Path was knowing where people stood, and who still had nowhere to put him. He had a partner at a lamp in the north window who had not yet decided what she thought of that friend, and was entitled to take her time.
  1967	
  1968	He blew out his own lamp, and lay down, and let the cold room be a cold room, and slept.

