+# Movement 004 cold-read review
+
+## Read status and coverage
+
+This is a **fresh-context simulated cold read**, not a real audience measurement. I read only the seven supplied manuscript chapters, Chapters 23–29, in order. No editorial canon evidence was supplied or consulted, so I can assess internal continuity and flag apparent inconsistencies, but I cannot verify canon violations, plan adherence, or reserved-disclosure boundaries against project files.
+
+The supplied movement reads as complete: anticipation leads into the Brom bout; the loss produces tactical, social, and emotional consequences; the new partnership develops through controlled testing; an Iron-adjacent fragment arrives; and the final chapter establishes its limits and the next line of work.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+My interest first caught on Cael discovering that nothing hurt, distrusting the fact, and reducing his safeguards to two lines. The sharper hook came when he looked at the Ironyard and thought, *I'm going to lose there tonight*, only to find that he felt hungry. That gives the long pre-bout preparation a concrete emotional shape. The coal-box egg, Lira's invented errands, the old man's appearance, and the many lamps then make the bout feel like a communal event rather than an isolated power test.
+
+The strongest sustained passage is the main-floor fight in Chapters 24–25. Its pleasure is not whether Cael will spring a surprise victory; the manuscript has prepared me to believe he will lose. The pleasure is watching each intelligent plan fail for a more intelligent reason. The post came back, the dock partner was heavy, Brom has already met speed, and damaged legs cannot deliver a full Wind burst. Each correction changes the available fight rather than merely adding explanation. Cael's one plain hit matters more because it does not reverse the result. Brom sitting beside him instead of standing over him is the emotional payoff of the contest.
+
+Interest slipped in two places. Chapter 23's procession of preparations, wagers, lamps, onlookers, and floor ritual is atmospheric and ultimately earned, but a movement-only reader has to retain many unnamed or lightly contextualized people before the central contest begins. More materially, the opening half of Chapter 25 replays a substantial portion of the bout just completed in Chapter 24. Brom's interpretation is valuable, but the repeated mechanics briefly reduce forward pull after the fight's best surprise.
+
+Cael matters most to me because he is rigorous without being emotionally bloodless. He treats a loss as information, protects his rule when breaking it would be easier, gives Keth only what is his to give, and recognizes that sharing the Log is a personal choice rather than a tactical transaction. Brom becomes nearly as important because his competence includes restraint and because he can learn from Cael without being diminished by him. Lira supplies the movement's emotional pressure: her care is practical, her distrust is allowed to remain unresolved, and her admission that she minded the Log being shared is unusually honest.
+
+I expect the next movement to put Cael on a main-season card while he tries to make the new read function around motion or multiple people. I also expect the unexplained third-exchange disappearance to return as a separate problem, and I expect the Cael–Brom friendship to test Lira's sense of what had belonged to the original partnership. I would continue without hesitation.
+
+## Fluent thirteen-year-old lens
+
+These scores are editorial judgments through a reading lens, not demographic findings.
+
+| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location | Reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 8 | Chapter 23, opening body inventory through “What he felt was hungry.” | The coming loss, the two written rules, and Cael's appetite create a clear promise. The initial injury inventory and unexplained terms delay the emotional hook slightly for a movement-only reader. | High |
+| Keep reading | 9 | Chapter 25, Vell records “Atypical movement, third exchange”; Chapter 29, final paragraph beginning “On Monday Dace would chalk the main season...” | The bout pays off locally while the disappearance, new fragment, main-season posting, and changing relationships all pull forward. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9 | Chapter 24, “The plan had lasted three blows”; Chapter 26, “Every ability has two ledgers”; Chapter 28, “FRAGMENT ACQUIRED.” | A protagonist loses decisively yet gains a friend, a better model, and later a limited ability. Progress comes through observation, consent, and cost rather than a surprise win. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 7 | Chapter 24, from “The recovery was real and he could not reach it” through the second-door test; Chapter 25 opening replay | The physical logic is unusually trackable, but a cold reader must learn *read*, *Pressure*, *Wind*, *giving face*, *hold*, *recovery*, and *purse* rapidly. The POV replay can momentarily make the timeline feel as though it has moved backward without enough new ground. | Medium-high |
+| Character attachment | 9 | Chapter 25, Brom sits beside Cael and introduces himself again; Chapter 26, Lira says, “I minded... and now I've said it” in substance through her response to the shared Log | Cael, Brom, and Lira make consequential, distinct choices. Care is shown through behavior rather than speeches, and nobody is made simple to protect Cael's feelings. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 8 | Chapter 23, the coal-box egg breakfast; Chapter 26, “Also: made a friend today”; Chapter 28, Brom laughing at “See the sheet” | The humor grows from specific habits and affection and gives relief without weakening the injuries or stakes. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 9 | Chapters 24–25, from “Brom did not move at the word” through “Hand up. Fourth exchange.” | Position, contact, adaptation, and accumulated damage remain legible. Every exchange discovers something new, and the loss is both earned and satisfying. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 9 | Chapter 27, Cael identifies the lean before Brom's turn; Chapter 28, acquisition notice; Chapter 29, first four-breath use and headache | Learning produces concrete new perception, a new limitation, and changed relationships. The fragment is exciting because it begins nearly useless. | High |
+| Connection | 9 | Chapter 23's two rules; Chapter 26's *Carrying* column; Chapter 29, “L., through the wall. Didn't.” | Rules, injuries, bread, lamps, the Log, and the idea of carrying recur with changed meaning. The movement rewards memory without depending on a hidden reveal. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 8 | Chapter 24, the lead-seam fall and “the floor's bill”; Chapter 29, the sequence of forty failed attempts | Repetition and cadence carry action well, and speakers are easy to follow. Some long analytical sentences stack clauses and metaphors densely enough that a listener may need a second pass. | Medium-high |
+
+## Adult genre-reader lens
+
+| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location | Reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 9 | Chapter 23, body inventory, the two-line rule, and “I'm going to lose there tonight” / “What he felt was hungry.” | The opening makes self-command, anticipated defeat, and bodily cost immediate before widening into the public event. | High |
+| Keep reading | 9 | Chapter 25, the handshake on the floor; Chapter 29, the first involuntary perception of Lira through the wall and the main-season hook | The movement completes several arcs while opening specific rather than generic next questions. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9 | Chapter 25, Brom's account of choosing sight over an emptied read; Chapter 26, the two-ledger insight; Chapter 28, sharing the whole Log | The tactical system and the ethics of studying another person are integrated. Brom is neither a wall to overcome nor a mentor dispensing effortless answers. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 9 | Chapter 24, each failed plan is restated as new arithmetic; Chapter 29, Cael's final six-field Log entry | The prose repeatedly converts abstract abilities into physical consequences and summarizes without flattening them. The Chapter 25 replay is the main pacing exception. | High |
+| Character attachment | 10 | Chapter 26, Brom's Velmere account and Cael's disclosure; Chapter 28, Lira's response to losing exclusivity over the Log | Vulnerability is reciprocal but not symmetrical. Each relationship changes through consent, boundaries, and uncomfortable truth rather than sentimental shorthand. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 9 | Chapter 23, breakfast and the invented errands; Chapter 27, Brom's first laugh; Chapter 28, the blush over “made a friend today” | Warmth accumulates through recurring behavior, and the rare laughs mark changes in intimacy. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 9 | Chapters 24–25, all four exchanges | The fight is long because its problem keeps changing. Brom adapts, Cael revises, the floor matters, injuries constrain later choices, and mercy emerges from tactical knowledge. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 9 | Chapter 27, the shift from bench knowledge to contact knowledge; Chapter 28, the Iron-adjacent acquisition; Chapter 29, “It doesn't aim.” | Advancement is earned through repeated embodied work, and the new fragment changes Cael's method rather than simply raising his power. | High |
+| Connection | 10 | From Chapter 23's “Two lines” to Chapter 29's *Carrying* entry and “Begin”; also the returning bread, lamps, wall, bench, and Log | Motifs and causal state carry exceptionally well across all seven chapters. Tactical details become relationship details and then thematic details without losing their literal function. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 9 | Chapter 25, “For half a beat, Brom believed the read and not his eyes”; Chapter 29, “There turned out to be a great many ways to fail at doing nothing.” | Sentence rhythm is controlled, dialogue voices are distinct, and repeated structures land cleanly. A few clause-heavy analytical paragraphs would benefit from light oral smoothing, not broad simplification. | High |
+
+## Internal continuity and disclosure check
+
+### Canon
+
+No canon packet or project evidence was supplied, so there are no verified canon contradictions to report. The literal token `[SHATTERED]` in Chapter 26 appears to be unresolved or protected manuscript text, but without canon I cannot determine the intended in-world word. It is a production-level reader interruption, not a canon finding.
+
+### Physical state
+
+The movement consistently carries bodily consequences. Cael's right arm goes numb in the first exchange; the reactive Wind burst widens the hip line; Brom damages both knees in the second and finishes through them in the fourth; the knees swell that night; Cael descends stairs seated the next morning; he uses the measuring stick; on the fourth day he tests the market steps without it; and a week after the bout the knee remains explicitly “bad.” Forearm bruising, swollen hands, shoulder pain, and the new-read headache also continue into later scenes.
+
+The one plausibility pressure point is the intensity of the fixed-cross work within a week of the bilateral knee injury, especially Chapter 28's full-force answers that turn Cael over the bad knee and throw him into a wall. The text mitigates this with negotiated stop terms, Cael's active checks, Brom's earlier caution, and the knee's continuing complaints. I read this as aggressive character choice rather than a clear state contradiction.
+
+### Power limits
+
+Limits are unusually well maintained. Cael refuses Pressure during the bout, later tests only a quarter-strength use, and receives the predicted returned cost in his shoulder. The Wind charges full price even when damaged legs provide only a third of the movement, so he shuts it. Brom's Iron Skin requires breath/read/contact logic, learns repeated attacks, and has a recovery window that his throws deliberately make inaccessible. The new Iron-adjacent read initially requires stillness, non-aiming attention, close range, a single strong target, and exclusivity from Cael's other fragments; it produces a disproportionate headache after only a few breaths.
+
+The third-exchange disappearance is internally treated as an unknown event with no sensed activation, cost, handle, or repeatability. The later Iron-adjacent acquisition has a notice, a surface sensation, a perceptual effect, and a measurable attention cost. Nothing proves they are the same ability, but their proximity and shared relation to Brom's read invite that inference strongly enough that some cold readers may temporarily conflate them.
+
+### Knowledge boundaries
+
+Knowledge moves through observation, disclosure, or explicit testing. Cael's pre-bout model is wrong exactly where bench observation cannot reveal Brom's adaptations. Brom knows Cael's public classification through talk and Vell's book, senses only what his read can detect, and sees the full Log only after Cael offers it. Cael honors Brom's privacy by giving Keth the general discovery about touch rather than Brom-specific technique. Lira learns about the new fragment and the shared Log directly from Cael. These boundaries are clear and dramatically productive.
+
+### Reserved disclosures
+
+Without editorial context I cannot verify what was reserved. Within the supplied manuscript, disclosure remains controlled: the cause of the third-exchange absence is withheld; Brom postpones part of the estate-wall history; the system behind Cael's fragments remains unnamed; Lira's private book is noticed but not opened; and the fragment's range in crowds or around unkindled people remains untested. I found no internal moment where a character reveals knowledge the movement has not shown them acquiring.
+
+## Repair brief
+
+1. **Location:** Chapter 26, market-wall conversation: “I'm `[SHATTERED]`, said Cael.” **Observed issue:** The bracketed all-caps token reads as an unresolved placeholder or redaction inside otherwise finished prose. **Effect on the reader:** It breaks immersion at the movement's most vulnerable reciprocal disclosure and makes the stigma Cael is choosing to name impossible to hear in-world. **Proposed scope:** Replace only this token with the owner-approved canonical term; do not infer or invent it during copy repair. **Strength to preserve:** Cael's agency in saying the word himself and Brom's unstartled “I know.”
+
+2. **Location:** Chapter 25, from “Brom had known what the boy was carrying from the first blow” through the return to the third exchange. **Observed issue:** The secondary POV restates much of Chapter 24's tactical sequence before reaching Brom-only information. **Effect on the reader:** Forward momentum dips immediately after the strongest surprise, and the same mechanics can feel explained twice. **Proposed scope:** Tighten only the already-visible chronology and retain Brom's exclusive discoveries: that he throws opponents beyond the gap deliberately, that he detects Cael holding Pressure shut, that Cael's absence destabilizes the read, and that Brom chooses not to force a recurrence. Preserve the full developed fight in Chapter 24. **Strength to preserve:** Brom's POV turns an apparent obstacle into a patient, ethical, adaptive person.
+
+3. **Location:** Chapter 25's third-exchange absence; Chapter 28's `FRAGMENT ACQUIRED` notice; Chapter 29's first controlled uses. **Observed issue:** The manuscript records important differences between the unexplained absence and the Iron-adjacent read, but never gives the cold reader one clean comparison after acquisition. **Effect on the reader:** The later fragment may be mistaken for an explanation or repeat of the earlier anomaly, weakening both the mystery and the precision of the progression system. **Proposed scope:** Add one brief comparison in Cael's Chapter 28 or Chapter 29 Log reasoning: the new fragment has surface sensation, perception, a notice, and an attention cost, while the third-exchange event had none. Do not identify or explain the earlier event. **Strength to preserve:** The anomaly remains genuinely unknown, and the acquired fragment begins limited, costly, and earned.

